article","created":{"date-parts":[[2005,7,22]],"date-time":"2005-07-22T18:01:52Z","timestamp":1122055312000},"page":"9-19","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["&lt;title&gt;The NASA search for evidence of technological civilizations in space: project HRMS&lt;\/title&gt;"],"prefix":"10.1117","volume":"1867","author":[{"given":"David H.","family":"Brocker","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Peter R.","family":"Backus","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"189","event":{"name":"OE\/LASE'93: Optics, Electro-Optics, & Laser Applications in Science& Engineering","location":"Los Angeles, CA"},"container-title":["SPIE Proceedings","The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence (SETI) in the Optical Spectrum"],"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/proceedings.spiedigitallibrary.org\/proceeding.aspx?articleid=1006374","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2017,3,6]],"date-time":"2017-03-06T21:26:29Z","timestamp":1488835589000},"score":34.19942,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"http:\/\/proceedings.spiedigitallibrary.org\/proceeding.aspx?articleid=1006374"}},"editor":[{"given":"Stuart A.","family":"Kingsley","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"editor","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[1993,8,6]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1117\/12.150124","ISSN":["0277-786X"],"issn-type":[{"type":"print","value":"0277-786X"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[1993,8,6]]}},{"institution":[{"name":"ScienceOpen"}],"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,10]],"date-time":"2026-06-10T10:02:58Z","timestamp":1781085778162,"version":"3.54.1"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,5]]},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"ScienceOpen","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,5]],"date-time":"2026-06-05T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1780617600000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>\n                  The existence of extraterrestrial intelligence remains one of the most profound questions in modern astrobiology and astronomy. Although advances in exoplanet discovery, planetary habitability studies, biosignature detection, and the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence (SETI) have greatly expanded our understanding of potentially life-supporting environments, no confirmed evidence of extraterrestrial civilizations has yet been obtained. This apparent contradiction between the abundance of potentially habitable worlds and the absence of confirmed contact is commonly known as the Fermi Paradox or the Great Silence. This review synthesizes current knowledge on extraterrestrial habitability, evolutionary pathways to intelligence, technosignatures, advanced technological civilizations, unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAPs), and future SETI strategies within a unified conceptual framework. The principal contribution is the development of the\n                  <jats:bold>Civilization Detectability\u2013Concealment Framework (CDCF)<\/jats:bold>\n                  , which treats detectability as a dynamic and evolving property of civilizations rather than a fixed consequence of their existence. The framework introduces three interconnected concepts. First, the\n                  <jats:bold>Detectability Window Hypothesis<\/jats:bold>\n                  proposes that civilizations may remain observable only during limited phases of technological development before transitioning toward more efficient and less detectable modes of communication or existence. Second, the\n                  <jats:bold>Asymmetric Awareness Hypothesis<\/jats:bold>\n                  suggests that significant technological disparities may allow advanced civilizations to detect emerging civilizations such as humanity without reciprocal detectability. Third, the\n                  <jats:bold>Concealment Spectrum Framework<\/jats:bold>\n                  examines how technological, environmental, and intentional factors may progressively reduce the observability of advanced civilizations. In addition, the review presents a structured scientific assessment of UAP observations, emphasizing the distinction between unexplained phenomena and evidence of extraterrestrial origin, and evaluates the possibility of extraterrestrial probes or long-duration technological artifacts within the Solar System based on current scientific knowledge. A multimodal SETI strategy is also proposed, integrating radio, optical, infrared, atmospheric, and artificial intelligence-assisted technosignature searches. The analysis suggests that the absence of confirmed extraterrestrial contact does not necessarily imply the absence of extraterrestrial intelligence. Instead, it may result from the combined effects of evolutionary uncertainty, limited detectability windows, concealment processes, technological asymmetry, and vast spatial and temporal separations. By integrating these factors within a single framework, the CDCF provides a new perspective on the Great Silence, identifies testable predictions, and offers a multidisciplinary foundation for future research in astrobiology, SETI, and technosignature science.\n                <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.14293\/pr2199.003782.v1","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,10]],"date-time":"2026-06-10T09:40:13Z","timestamp":1781084413000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Extraterrestrial Civilizations and the Great Silence: A Detectability\u2013Concealment Framework for Understanding Alien Evolution, Technological Signatures, and the Fermi Paradox"],"prefix":"10.14293","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-6141-0545","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Aswin","family":"Karkadakattil","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"5403","link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/scienceopen.com\/hosted-document?doi=10.14293\/PR2199.003782.v1","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,10]],"date-time":"2026-06-10T09:40:13Z","timestamp":1781084413000},"score":31.652555,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/scienceopen.com\/hosted-document?doi=10.14293\/PR2199.003782.v1"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,5]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.14293\/pr2199.003782.v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,5]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2022,10,11]],"date-time":"2022-10-11T11:16:02Z","timestamp":1665486962232},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"Berghahn Books","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,31]]},"DOI":"10.1515\/9780857452122-toc","type":"book-chapter","created":{"date-parts":[[2022,10,11]],"date-time":"2022-10-11T10:57:11Z","timestamp":1665485831000},"page":"vii-viii","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["CONTENTS"],"prefix":"10.1515","member":"374","container-title":["Civilizations Beyond Earth"],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.degruyter.com\/document\/doi\/10.1515\/9780857452122-toc\/xml","content-type":"application\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.degruyter.com\/document\/doi\/10.1515\/9780857452122-toc\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2022,10,11]],"date-time":"2022-10-11T10:57:24Z","timestamp":1665485844000},"score":29.076202,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.degruyter.com\/document\/doi\/10.1515\/9780857452122-toc\/html"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,31]]},"references-count":0,"alternative-id":["10.1515\/9780857452122-toc","10.1515\/9780857452122"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9780857452122-toc","published":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,31]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,8]],"date-time":"2026-06-08T22:57:18Z","timestamp":1780959438665,"version":"3.54.1"},"reference-count":57,"publisher":"American Astronomical Society","issue":"2","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,15]],"date-time":"2026-04-15T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1776211200000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,15]],"date-time":"2026-04-15T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1776211200000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/iopscience.iop.org\/info\/page\/text-and-data-mining"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["iopscience.iop.org"],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["ApJ"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,20]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>One of the most interesting questions that astronomy can hope to answer is: Are we alone in our Milky Way Galaxy? A detection of an electromagnetic (EM) signal generated by an extraterrestrial technological intelligence, or the presence in our solar system of an alien probe, would answer this question in the negative. Purposeful interstellar communication is a two-way street\u2014the transmitting and receiving technological intelligences (TIs) both need to do their parts. As the receiving TI, our EM search programs should incorporate a model of what a transmitting TI is likely to be doing. Published works on the search for extraterrestrial technological intelligence (SETI) have generally not done so, and thus have often been suboptimally designed. We propose an improved search technique that more closely corresponds to astronomical surveys that have been undertaken for reasons that have nothing to do with SETI. Published non-SETI radio and optical surveys are sufficiently extensive that they already supply meaningful constraints on the prevalence of nearby, purposely communicative alien civilizations. Purposeful communication can also include the sending of spaceships (probes). The absence of evidence for alien probes in the solar system suggests that no alien civilization has passed within \u223c100 lt-yr of Earth during the past few billion years.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3847\/1538-4357\/ae4c38","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,15]],"date-time":"2026-04-15T07:49:59Z","timestamp":1776239399000},"page":"167","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1088\/crossmark-policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":1,"title":["Broadband Searches for Extraterrestrial Technological Intelligence: A New Strategy to Find Nearby Alien Civilizations"],"prefix":"10.3847","volume":"1001","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6809-3045","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"B.","family":"Zuckerman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"2208","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,15]]},"reference":[{"key":"apjae4c38bib1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"35","DOI":"10.3847\/1538-4365\/ac4414","type":"journal-article","volume":"259","author":"Abdurro\u2019uf","year":"2022","journal-title":"ApJS"},{"key":"apjae4c38bib2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"43","DOI":"10.3847\/1538-3881\/ab9813","type":"journal-article","volume":"160","author":"Anguiano","year":"2020","journal-title":"AJ"},{"key":"apjae4c38bib3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"559","DOI":"10.1086\/176166","type":"journal-article","volume":"450","author":"Becker","year":"1995","journal-title":"ApJ"},{"key":"apjae4c38bib4","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"623","DOI":"10.1016\/0094-5765(86)90052-4","type":"journal-article","volume":"13","author":"Betz","year":"1986","journal-title":"AcAau"},{"key":"apjae4c38bib5","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"